  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 1244 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nA warming trend is forecast from early next week through mid-week\nas the core of ridging redevelops closer to the forecast area. \nFor Monday, highs only a few degrees warmer than this weekend are \nforecast, with exception of 105F-110F reading for the Big Bend, \nbut by Tuesday and Wednesday, highs rise into the 100F-105F range \nfor much of the northern and central Permian Basin, Stockton \nPlateau, and SE NM plains, while highs rise into the 105F-110F \nrange along parts of the Pecos River valley and into the 95F-100F \nrange even for higher elevations. Wednesday is forecast to be the \nwarmest day in the Long Term period, with both highs and heat \nindices rising close to if not exceeding hazardous heat criteria \nover parts of the Eddy County Plains, northeast Permian Basin, and\nDavis and Chinati Mountains as well as Big Bend. However, as the \nmid-level ridging develops to the east of the area and allows for \nsoutherly return flow and increased moisture aloft and at the \nsurface (evidenced by PWATs increasing back into the 1.00\" to \n1.25\" range in ensembles) and a strengthening and eastward \ndeveloping surface lee trough, low to medium (25% to 35%) \nshower/storm chances will again return to the western higher \nterrain into Presidio Valley. Low to medium (25% to 35%) rain \nchances expand east into the northwest Permian Basin into Stockton\nPlateau and Big Bend by the end of the week, while increased \ncloud cover limiting diurnal heating pushes highs back down to the\n100F-105F range in the warmest spots of the lowlands and 90F-95F \nrange for higher elevations Thursday, 95F-100F lowlands and \n85F-905F range for higher elevations Friday through early next \nweekend. Lows next week will remain in the 65F-75F range for the \nlowlands and 60F-65F range for the Marfa Plateau as amount of \novernight cooling is not expected to be significantly affected by \nreturn moisture. Rainfall amounts and storm coverage/timing each \nday during the latter half of next week next also remain highly \nuncertain at this time.",
